WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.740
- I do love talking science with you,

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.473
because I don't know Jack about it.

00:00:03.473 --> 00:00:05.040
- That means you're still curious,

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.970
and you preserved that into adulthood.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:08.370
- I actually, this is even ready,

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:09.990
but I bought your book
last week on Amazon.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.200
I haven't been able to read it yet.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.290
Not because you were coming on.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:14.340
I actually didn't even know that then.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.382
I just love you.

00:00:15.382 --> 00:00:16.506
I think you're so--
- Oh, oh, okay.

00:00:16.506 --> 00:00:17.339
(audience applauds)

00:00:17.339 --> 00:00:18.172
Okay.
- Yes.

00:00:18.172 --> 00:00:20.318
His new book is "Letters
from an Astrophysicist."

00:00:20.318 --> 00:00:21.618
it's so cool.

00:00:21.618 --> 00:00:22.940
It's cool.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:24.155
So, tell me about science.

00:00:24.155 --> 00:00:25.520
(laughs)

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:26.740
- Help me.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:27.573
Help me.

00:00:27.573 --> 00:00:31.947
- Okay, so scientists are
kids who never grew up.

00:00:31.947 --> 00:00:32.972
- Oh, that's what artists are.

00:00:32.972 --> 00:00:33.805
- Yeah.

00:00:33.805 --> 00:00:34.638
(audience laughs)

00:00:34.638 --> 00:00:35.900
Yeah, and what are kids
that you're turning,

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.860
what's under here and what's this,

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.110
and they're taking things apart.

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:41.880
They're basically
experimenting, and as parents,

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.080
we tend to over constrain
their experiments.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:46.980
You spend the first year of
their lives teaching them

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.300
to walk and talk, and the rest

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.010
of their lives telling them
to shut up and sit down.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:50.843
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:50.843 --> 00:00:51.676
- Am I right or wrong?

00:00:51.676 --> 00:00:52.509
That's what you do, all right?

00:00:52.509 --> 00:00:53.342
- Were you in my house?
- Guilty!

00:00:53.342 --> 00:00:54.480
(audience laughs)
- Yeah, but I need you

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.313
to explain a few things,

00:00:55.313 --> 00:00:56.840
like sonic boom, go.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:57.790
- Love me some sonic boom.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:58.690
- Okay.
- Oh.

00:00:58.690 --> 00:00:59.836
- I love Sonic.
- Okay.

00:00:59.836 --> 00:01:02.090
(audience laughs)

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:03.380
- They got a lot of drink
options, that's real nice.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:04.920
- Sonic shakes, yeah, yeah.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.180
So when you sing, when
any of us makes any sound,

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:12.050
the sound emanates at the
speed of sound from you, okay?

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:14.230
Now, suppose you start moving.

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:16.380
Well, the sound that's in front

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.770
of you is not leaving you as
fast as the sound behind you,

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.011
and there's a point
where you move so fast--

00:01:23.011 --> 00:01:24.130
(imitates explosion)

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.000
Wait, wait, I haven't even
gotten to that part yet.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.270
You move so fast

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:31.390
that the sound that you
make does not advance

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.430
in front of you at all.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:34.372
- What?

00:01:34.372 --> 00:01:35.280
(audience laughs)

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.970
- Because you're going
the speed of sound itself.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:38.803
- Oh, so you're

00:01:38.803 --> 00:01:39.636
going faster than it.
- So when you try

00:01:39.636 --> 00:01:41.310
to make a sound, and
you go faster than it,

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.290
all your sound goes behind you,

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:47.160
and all that sound gathers
up on a shock wave,

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.040
and that shock wave, if it's an airplane,

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:50.440
is moving through the air,

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:53.630
and by the time it hits the
ground, it'll (claps hands) bam!

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:54.672
That's the sonic boom.
- What?

00:01:54.672 --> 00:01:55.505
Oh, that's what you're saying, okay.

00:01:55.505 --> 00:01:58.040
- Because it's faster than
the sound that it generated.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.730
- Wow, I wish I'd had a

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:00.850
science teacher like you.
- By the way, the sonic booms

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.300
in our lives, the crack of
a whip, that's a sonic boom.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.133
The tip of the whip.

00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:04.966
- The tip of the whip.

00:02:04.966 --> 00:02:06.380
- [Neil] Yes, yes, yes.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:07.810
- [Kelly] That's amazing.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:09.168
- Is every sentence musical to you?

00:02:09.168 --> 00:02:10.533
(audience laughs)
- Every time, every time.

00:02:10.533 --> 00:02:11.660
- The tip of the whip.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:12.550
- Black holes.

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:13.651
- I'll try to give you some more of that

00:02:13.651 --> 00:02:14.484
by the end of the show.

00:02:14.484 --> 00:02:15.640
- I do want to know about black holes.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:16.845
- Oh, okay, you didn't
learn about black holes?

00:02:16.845 --> 00:02:18.400
- No, I didn't learn,

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:19.970
'cause I felt like it was wasted on me.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:21.690
As a kid, you were kind of
like, it's over your head,

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:22.523
and now I'm an adult, and I'm into it.

00:02:22.523 --> 00:02:23.788
- Number one, avoid them.

00:02:23.788 --> 00:02:24.621
(audience laughs)
- Avoid them.

00:02:24.621 --> 00:02:26.613
Well, who knows what's in them?

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:27.520
Want to go find out?

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.200
- Matthew McConaughey
did in "Interstellar."

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:30.663
I'm just saying, it works out.

00:02:30.663 --> 00:02:31.496
- If you go inside a black hole,

00:02:31.496 --> 00:02:34.080
you will not ever come out
to tell us what was there.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.370
- But where will you go?

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:36.460
- Well, okay.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.320
(audience laughs)

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:39.330
- Where will we go?

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:40.253
- Okay, all right, so--

00:02:40.253 --> 00:02:42.453
- If I didn't have children,
I would totally go.

00:02:42.453 --> 00:02:43.706
- I will tell you, bring 'em.

00:02:43.706 --> 00:02:44.539
(audience laughs)

00:02:44.539 --> 00:02:46.927
- No, just in case it's not good.

00:02:46.927 --> 00:02:47.970
I'll suffer on my own.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:48.803
- Here's what happens.

00:02:48.803 --> 00:02:49.860
As you get near a black hole,

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.840
the difference in
gravity between your head

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.830
and your feet begins to grow,

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:55.740
which means your feet will fall towards

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.140
the black hole faster than your head will.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:02:58.973
- Will I get taller?

00:02:58.973 --> 00:03:00.593
(audience laughs)
- Yes, yes.

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:02.432
- I'm in.

00:03:02.432 --> 00:03:07.000
- However, the getting
taller part will never cease.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:07.833
- [Kelly] Wow.

00:03:07.833 --> 00:03:09.840
- And so, it'll continue,
the whole tidal forces,

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.260
and your body will be
stretched to the point

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:16.380
where the tidal forces exceed
the intermolecular forces

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:18.330
that hold your flesh together,

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:21.970
and so then your body will snap in two,

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:25.020
likely at the base of your
waist, at the base of your spine,

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.670
then those two parts--

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:27.570
- Like that "Saw" movie.

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:28.640
(audience laughs)
- keep falling in

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.380
and then those two parts feel the forces,

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.570
and they split into two
pieces, and then four,

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:35.880
and then eight, and you will
bifurcate all the way down

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.200
to this cosmic abyss.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.738
- Where does the day bifurcate?

00:03:38.738 --> 00:03:40.696
(audience laughs)
- Bifurcate, and that's not

00:03:40.696 --> 00:03:42.258
even the worst of it.
(audience applauds)

00:03:42.258 --> 00:03:43.091
- What?

00:03:43.091 --> 00:03:45.860
- Okay, that's not even the worst.

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:47.027
- We haven't gotten to the worst part.

00:03:47.027 --> 00:03:48.240
- But the worst part is--

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:49.270
- That was the fun part.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:52.010
- The fabric of space and time narrows.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.569
It funnels down to a point,

00:03:53.569 --> 00:03:58.569
so you have a certain width
up here, and you get extruded.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:03:59.953
- Oh, like my waist.

00:03:59.953 --> 00:04:00.786
- To the fabric of space.

00:04:00.786 --> 00:04:03.420
Like toothpaste through a tube.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:06.210
So if you die in that way,
we have a word for that.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.764
It's called spaghettification.

00:04:07.764 --> 00:04:08.676
(audience laughs)

00:04:08.676 --> 00:04:09.509
- Is that a real word?

00:04:09.509 --> 00:04:10.390
- Yes, it is a real word.

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:11.223
Look it up.

00:04:12.102 --> 00:04:14.370
I composed a poem about black holes.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:15.203
- Do it.

00:04:15.203 --> 00:04:16.036
Yes.

00:04:16.036 --> 00:04:17.420
- Just to summarize what you just learned.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:18.253
- That is terrifying.

00:04:18.253 --> 00:04:19.500
- Are you ready?

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:20.830
- Yeah.

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:25.320
- In this feet-first dive
to this cosmic abyss,

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.230
you will not survive
because you will not miss.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:33.010
The tidal forces of gravity
will create quite a calamity

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:35.270
when you're stretched head to toe.

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:37.460
Are you sure you want to go?

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:39.980
Your body's patterns, you'll see them,

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:42.530
will enter one-by-one.

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:47.054
The event horizon will eat 'em,
and you won't be having fun.

00:04:47.054 --> 00:04:48.896
- No, Neil, it's terrifying.

00:04:48.896 --> 00:04:50.055
(audience laughs)

00:04:50.055 --> 00:04:53.623
What the hell just happened?
(audience applauds)

00:04:53.623 --> 00:04:54.820
I have a question I wanted to ask you.

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:56.040
I think you've already answered it.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:56.873
- What's that?

00:04:56.873 --> 00:04:58.420
- What's the scariest
thing about the universe?

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:00.050
- Well, it's not falling
into a black hole.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:01.286
- That's not the worst?

00:05:01.286 --> 00:05:02.510
(audience laughs)

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:05.170
- No, no, there's way
scarier stuff than that.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:08.740
In five billion years, the
Sun will become bloated.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:10.339
It will expand so large--

00:05:10.339 --> 00:05:11.661
- I get that.
that it--

00:05:11.661 --> 00:05:13.783
(audience laughs)

00:05:13.783 --> 00:05:14.866
- I get that.

00:05:16.347 --> 00:05:17.422
I expand every month.

00:05:17.422 --> 00:05:18.255
- [Neil] I love this show.

00:05:18.255 --> 00:05:21.850
Every month it just gets bigger,
and then it goes back down.

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:22.910
Okay, go ahead.

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:26.017
- So, how can I continue now?

00:05:26.017 --> 00:05:27.388
- 'Cause I'm bloated right now.

00:05:27.388 --> 00:05:28.846
This dress is tight.

00:05:28.846 --> 00:05:30.300
Okay, okay.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.710
- All right, the Sun,
in five billion years,

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:36.410
will bloat to become so large,

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:41.410
it will engulf the entire
orbits of Mercury and Venus.

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:44.120
The surface will be so near to Earth

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:47.640
that Earth's temperature
will rise exponentially,

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.010
and the oceans will come to a rolling boil

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:52.000
as they evaporate into the atmosphere,

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.690
then the atmosphere will
evaporate into space,

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:57.697
and we will be this charred
ember as we get absorbed

00:05:57.697 --> 00:06:00.917
by the Sun, descending into the crucible

00:06:00.917 --> 00:06:02.970
that is its burning center.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:05.220
- Holy what?

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:06.140
- Have a nice day.
(audience laughs)

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:06.973
- Yeah!

00:06:06.973 --> 00:06:07.806
(laughs)

00:06:07.806 --> 00:06:08.860
And there's no Segway here,

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:11.014
I just want to kind of gear
us away from terrifying.

00:06:11.014 --> 00:06:11.960
(audience laughs)

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.960
So, when I did your show
a couple of years ago,

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.961
we talked about the
great American eclipse.

00:06:16.961 --> 00:06:17.794
- Yeah.

00:06:17.794 --> 00:06:19.580
- Which I actually saw at my farm.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:21.820
- I was so jealous, 'cause
I spent all this money

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:23.020
to go to a mountaintop,

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:26.262
and I could have just been
invited to your backyard.

00:06:26.262 --> 00:06:27.760
(audience laughs)
Is my invitation still

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:28.593
in my inbox?

00:06:28.593 --> 00:06:29.426
- Oh, my gosh!

00:06:29.426 --> 00:06:30.730
- Did I miss the invitation?

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:32.510
- No, honestly, it was
a last-minute thing.

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:33.580
We were supposed to--

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:35.000
- Eclipses are not last-minute things!

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:35.833
(audience laughs)

00:06:35.833 --> 00:06:37.630
People plan their whole
life around eclipses!

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:38.474
- I know, a lot

00:06:38.474 --> 00:06:39.307
of people like me.
- This ain't a barbecue.

00:06:39.307 --> 00:06:40.140
Oops, there's an eclipse!

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:42.982
Was that what happened in
your backyard in Tennessee?

00:06:42.982 --> 00:06:44.923
- It really was, it was on our farm.

00:06:44.923 --> 00:06:46.290
- Y'all need some scientists down there.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:47.137
- Everybody was like, "Oh, we should go

00:06:47.137 --> 00:06:48.160
"to your farm and see the eclipse."

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:50.243
We were like, "Okay,"
and when we got there,

00:06:50.243 --> 00:06:52.242
gone to the gas station, got some glasses,

00:06:52.242 --> 00:06:53.210
it was awesome.
- Okay, all right.

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:55.560
- And all the animals,
everything started making noise.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:56.650
The crickets, everything started,

00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:57.760
and they thought it was nighttime.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:00.340
- And the craziest behaving
animals are the humans.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:03.041
- We are pretty nuts,
but we were drinking too.

00:07:03.041 --> 00:07:04.338
(audience laughs)

00:07:04.338 --> 00:07:07.390
But wait, I want to leave
everyone with a little hope,

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:10.420
okay, 'cause we've gone a
little zero dark thirty,

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:13.860
and I'm scared to walk
out of my house now,

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:15.210
and I never want to go near a black hole,

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:17.080
so just maybe give us something hopeful.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:19.470
- Some of the universe
derived from a cosmic--

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:22.000
- Something beautiful that makes us smile.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:27.000
- The atoms of your body
are traceable to events

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:30.350
that have long ago
occurred in the universe,

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:33.730
especially stars that have
manufactured these elements

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:37.590
and exploded, scattering that
enrichment across the galaxy,

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:41.810
enabling planets to
form, and life upon them,

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:44.780
so when you stand outside
and look up at the night sky,

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:47.600
it's not we're here and
the stars are there,

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:52.390
we are participant in this
great unfolding cosmic journey,

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:55.020
and it is not just figuratively true,

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:57.964
it is literally true that we are stardust.

00:07:57.964 --> 00:07:58.797
(Kelly gasps)

00:07:58.797 --> 00:07:59.630
- So we're all stars.

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:00.940
(audience laughs)

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:03.580
- It is not that we are in the universe.

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:06.780
That's true, but what's even more true is

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:08.441
that the universe is in us.

00:08:08.441 --> 00:08:09.298
(gasps)

00:08:09.298 --> 00:08:11.619
(audience applauds)

00:08:11.619 --> 00:08:13.417
- I totally want to name my next album

00:08:13.417 --> 00:08:15.040
"The Universe Within Me."

